<!--Post: 11937--><!--Time: 1111441401-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11937"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Megabyte<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Megabyte<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, March 21, 2005 - 1:43 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The people who now currently inhabit the Old Otero Home say that house is not the same as what it was, I beg to differ, yes it might not have the same physical features as it did in the 70&#39;s and I think the pipe in the basement is the same one that Josephine was hung from, even though the house is not physically the same, it is spiritually the same, what goes on in a house stays in a house.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
